            "content": [
              {
                "type": "output_text",
                "text": "Using Bing grounding, the latest major news about NASA’s Mars Perseverance rover is:\n\n- **Perseverance has found evidence of ancient asteroid impacts on Mars.** NASA reported that the rover studied a 245-foot/75-meter-thick stack of layered rock on Jezero Crater’s rim, called the **“Broom Point member,”** which appears to have formed through repeated asteroid impacts more than **3.9 billion years ago**. This could preserve a rare record of the early solar system that Earth has largely lost due to plate tectonics.【6:1†source】\n\n- **The rover is also returning new views from “Broom Point.”** NASA recently highlighted a sweeping Mastcam-Z view looking back toward the outside lip of Jezero Crater’s rim, helping scientists understand the layered bedrock and terrain Perseverance is exploring.【6:1†source】\n\n- **Perseverance recently hit a distance milestone: a “marathon” on Mars.** NASA said the rover has driven **26.2 miles / 42.195 km** across Mars, reaching that mark on its **1,890th Martian day** of the mission. That took about five years and four months—less than half the time Opportunity needed to cover the same distance.【6:0†source】\n\n- **NASA’s Mars Reconnaissance Orbiter captured Perseverance from orbit** as a tiny speck, along with its tracks, near the time of that milestone; the rover was operating west of Jezero Crater in an area nicknamed **“Arbot.”**【6:0†source】\n\nIn short: Perseverance is healthy and active, has completed a marathon-distance traverse, and is now examining some of the oldest and most scientifically valuable rocks it has encountered—especially at Broom Point on Jezero Crater’s rim.",
                "annotations": [
